#!/usr/bin/python3
"""
wake-page: a small HTTPS page, behind a password, that shows whether the
home's computers are on, for how long, and wakes them. Devices are added and
removed on the page itself. See README, The wake page. Standard library only;
wake-page.service runs it as dietpi.

Wake-on-LAN is a broadcast on the home network, and Tailscale does not carry
broadcasts, so this laptop, which is always on, sends it when the button is
pressed: at home, or away over the tailnet.

"On" and "for how long" are what the laptop sees. It checks each device every
CHECK_SECS, by ping or, for one that ignores ping (a firewall, like the PC's),
by the kernel's ARP table: a device that is on answers ARP whatever its
firewall says. The time on counts from the first check that saw it on. While
the desktop is on, the page also asks whether Nextcloud and Open WebUI answer,
so it shows when the desktop is ready to use, and it can shut the desktop down:
an SSH login with the page's own key, which the desktop allows to run nothing
but its shutdown.

    wake-page                  serve on port 8443
    wake-page --set-password   ask for the password; only a hash is kept

The certificate is the laptop's one for all the home sites, from
~/.config/home-tls (home-certs makes it). The page's own files are in
~/.config/wake-page (700): machines.json (the devices, added, edited and
removed on the page; the first start writes DEFAULT_MACHINES into it),
password (a scrypt hash), secret (signs the login cookie), state.json (what
the checks saw), shutdown_key (the shut-down login) and known_hosts.
"""
